{"kind":"assert","text":"RULING on the current-seat authority boundary (request fe14f1d2), from inspection of chess.go:512-557 and the identity.Resolution surface. The two offered options are not alternatives: both halves are required, and neither alone is lawful. Fold-owned projection state cannot answer anchored recovery for a browser fingerprint that has never appended a record — seatSide begins with identities.LookupAt(record.ID), and an unknown fingerprint resolves to the zero Resolved, so recovery would silently never fire; filling that gap inside chess means re-deriving resolution, the second interpreter one layer down. A public identity query alone is worse: seat.identity, seat.anchored and Projection.identities are unexported, so the adapter would have to re-implement six rules in order — exact-key precedence (chess.go:540), the anchored/unanchored branch (543-550), the chess-scope test (560), collision (553), sameIdentity scheme+subject-only (564), and the both-or-neither ambiguity guard (516) — which is exactly the defect in miniature that the WIP liveRole (web.go:710-719) already exhibits: raw White/Black equality that calls an anchored-recovery key a watcher and a revoked-anchor key a player, and cannot express collision at all. THE CONTRACT. (1) Core identity gains one method, and only this shape: LookupActorAt(actor, recordID string) Resolved — the record ID names the instant, the actor names the key; it wraps the existing private lookup(actor, at), returns the already-public Resolved, and exports no new type. Any shape taking a position or timestamp is refused: it would export instant and leak the ordering model. (2) Chess Projection gains one exported non-mutating method, SeatFor(gameID, fingerprint string) (side string, ok bool), implemented by re-signing seatSide/matchSeat to take (actor string, resolved identity.Resolved) — mechanical, both already non-mutating — with resolution obtained via LookupActorAt at the frontier instant. (3) The frontier instant is chosen INSIDE the fold: the last record of the log names it, chess supplies it, and the adapter passes only a game ID and a fingerprint. The adapter never composes identity queries and never sees an instant. (4) Documented caveat, in the SeatFor doc comment: the fold has no clock, so the query judges at the last record instant — position-exact, timestamp-optimistic. The query is a preview and the durable append is the judgment; the query may say yes where a later append refuses on expiry, never the reverse. (5) Seat upgrade stays where it is: commit() runs only on the fold path after an act is otherwise accepted (chess.go:321, 353, 386, 411); SeatFor mutates nothing, preserving what chess_test.go:806, 865 and 927 pin. (6) The adapter deletes liveRole and the hand-rolled turn map in validateMotion (web.go:747) — SeatFor returns the fold own white/black vocabulary and the comparison target comes from the same fold, so the transcription disappears rather than being kept in sync. TESTS. In chess_test.go, equivalence tests reusing the existing seat-authority repos: for each scenario (anchored recovery at exact record order, expiry, wrong scope, collision, exact-key precedence, ambiguity), assert SeatFor for the fingerprint equals the verdict the fold reached for that fingerprint actual record — equivalence, not transcription, so it cannot drift from seatSide. A mutation of any of the six matchSeat rules must fail both arms. In cmd/chess/main_test.go, adapter tests assert only that the session and motion handlers obtain authority from SeatFor, not what the answer is. ARCHITECTURE: no contract change to the fold-is-sole-interpreter rule — this ruling enforces it; README durable-model section gains the preview-versus-judgment sentence. SEQUENCING: two child implementation requests follow — the core LookupActorAt in the core workroom, and the chess SeatFor plus adapter rewiring here, the latter to land inside the item-5 lane before its artifact and review are filed."}